I'll solve this directly using coordinate geometry.

**Proof.**

Place the triangle in coordinates with $B=(-1,0)$, $C=(1,0)$, $A=(0,h)$ for some $h>0$. Then $AB=AC=s$ where $s=\sqrt{1+h^{2}}$.

**Incenter and circle.** The incenter lies on the axis of symmetry at $I=(0,r)$, where $r=\dfrac{h}{1+s}$ (Area $=h$, semiperimeter $=1+s$). The circle through $B,C,I$ has center $(0,k)$ on the $y$-axis; equating $1+k^{2}=(r-k)^{2}$ gives $k=\dfrac{r^{2}-1}{2r}$.

**Key identity $hk=-1$.** Since $r^{2}-1=\dfrac{h^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}}-1=\dfrac{h^{2}-(1+s)^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}}=\dfrac{-2(1+s)}{(1+s)^{2}}=\dfrac{-2}{1+s}$, we get
$$hk=h\cdot\frac{r^{2}-1}{2r}=h\cdot\frac{-2/(1+s)}{2h/(1+s)}=-1.$$

**Circle equation.** A point $D=(x,y)$ on the circle satisfies
$$x^{2}+y^{2}-2ky=1,\quad\text{i.e.,}\quad x^{2}+y^{2}-1=2ky.\tag{$\ast$}$$

**Locating $E$.** The line through $C$ parallel to $BD$ has direction $(x+1,y)$, so $E=C+t(x+1,y)=(1+t(x+1),\,ty)$. Since $E$ also lies on line $AD$, write $E=A+\lambda(D-A)=(\lambda x,\,h+\lambda(y-h))$. Equating coordinates:
$$\lambda x=1+t(x+1),\qquad ty=h+\lambda(y-h).$$
Solving gives
$$\lambda=\frac{(x+1)h+y}{(x+1)h-y},\qquad t=\frac{(x-1)h+y}{(x+1)h-y}.$$

**Sign of $t$.** For $D$ inside $\triangle ABC$: the side $AC$ has equation $y=h(1-x)$, so the interior gives $y<(1-x)h$, hence $(x-1)h+y<0$. Similarly $y<(1+x)h$ gives $(x+1)h-y>0$. Thus $t<0$, so $|t|=-t$.

**The target identity.** Since $CE=|t|\cdot BD$ and $BD^{2}=(x+1)^{2}+y^{2}$, $CD^{2}=(x-1)^{2}+y^{2}$, the desired $CD^{2}=BD\cdot CE$ is equivalent to
$$|t|=\frac{(x-1)^{2}+y^{2}}{(x+1)^{2}+y^{2}},\quad\text{i.e.,}\quad t\bigl[(x+1)^{2}+y^{2}\bigr]+\bigl[(x-1)^{2}+y^{2}\bigr]=0.\tag{$\ast\ast$}$$

Substituting $t=\dfrac{(x-1)h+y}{(x+1)h-y}$, the left side of $(\ast\ast)$ becomes
$$\bigl[(x-1)h+y\bigr]\bigl[(x+1)^{2}+y^{2}\bigr]+\bigl[(x+1)h-y\bigr]\bigl[(x-1)^{2}+y^{2}\bigr].$$

Setting $a=x-1$, $b=x+1$ (so $a+b=2x$, $b-a=2$, $ab=x^{2}-1$), this equals
\begin{align*}
&(ah+y)(b^{2}+y^{2})+(bh-y)(a^{2}+y^{2})\\
&=h(ab^{2}+a^{2}b)+hy^{2}(a+b)+y(b^{2}-a^{2})\\
&=(a+b)\bigl[hab+hy^{2}+y(b-a)\bigr]\\
&=2x\bigl[h(x^{2}-1+y^{2})+2y\bigr]\\
&=2x\bigl[h(x^{2}+y^{2}-1)+2y\bigr].
\end{align*}
By $(\ast)$, $x^{2}+y^{2}-1=2ky$, so
$$h(x^{2}+y^{2}-1)+2y=2hky+2y=2y(hk+1)=2y\cdot 0=0,$$
using $hk=-1$. Therefore $(\ast\ast)$ holds, giving $|t|=\dfrac{CD^{2}}{BD^{2}}$, and hence
$$CD^{2}=|t|\cdot BD^{2}=BD\cdot CE. \qquad\blacksquare$$

